The Story Behind the
Estate Sale House =E2=80=94 in four parts

Part Two

"Mrs. Cole"

That the Maybeck Studio has survived so beautifully to this day=2C we have=
 the second owner to thank. There have been three owners of the Maybeck St=
udio at 2711 Buena Vista Way during its 102 years of existence; all owners=
 have either themselves been Maybecks or have been close friends of the Ma=
ybeck family. Frances van Barneveld Cole (1898=E2=80=931989) was the secon=
d owner.

Frances van Barneveld Cole. Water Brockmann=2C 1950

Her connection to the Maybecks went back to 1915 when her father was appoi=
nted Chief of the Department of Mining and Metallurgy (to be housed in the=
 Palace of the same name) at the Panama-Pacific International Exposition.=
 Also teaching courses at the University=2C Prof. Van Barneveld moved his=
 family to Berkeley where they landed in the neighborhood of Annie and Ber=
nard Maybeck=2C with the two families becoming friends. The four van Barne=
veld children=E2=80=94all teenagers=E2=80=94were close in age to Kerna and=
 Wallen Maybeck. Frances van Barneveld remembered planting redwood seedlin=
gs on the bare hillsides with Kerna and Wallen. The van Barnevelds stayed=
 in Berkeley long enough for the children to attend the University. Daught=
er Frances then spent most of her adult life in St Louis=2C married to Bar=
zillai Linneas Cole=2C the founder of the Cole Chemical Company=2C a pharm=
aceutical firm.

Frances Cole kept in touch with the Maybecks. In fact=2C the Maybecks stay=
ed with the Coles when Maybeck was asked to design a campus for Principia=
 College across the river at Elsah=2C Illinois. Mrs. Cole noticed that Ben=
 came down for breakfast each morning wearing a long velvet bathrobe of a=
 distinctive dark red color. That image stayed in her mind=2C and years la=
ter she utilized her memory of Maybeck=E2=80=99s sartorial preference when=
 she had dark red linen velvet draperies made for the Studio. On one of Ma=
ybeck=E2=80=99s many visits to St. Louis=2C the Coles commissioned him to=
 design a new building for Cole Chemical Company. The plans and rendering=
 still exist=2C but a local architect was ultimately chosen for practicali=
ty.

Maga and her parents in Palm Beach=2C c. 1936. Siegfried Brockmann papers

The Coles had one child: Margaret (1927=E2=80=931984)=2C whom her parents=
 were devoted to=2C and who was forever known by her childhood nickname=2C=
 =E2=80=9CMaga.=E2=80=9D In 1958=2C Maga decided to continue her study of=
 architecture in Berkeley. Her mother suggested she call on the Maybecks.=
 Wallen and his wife Jacomena were pleased to meet the next Cole generatio=
n (their own twins were the same age as Maga)=2C and they immediately rent=
ed their Maybeck Twin Drive cottage=2C Arillaga=2C to Maga. Mr. Cole died=
 unexpectedly in 1963. Mrs. Cole sold the company=2C as well as their St.=
 Louis estate=2C and headed west to be near Maga.

Maga at the time she left for Berkeley. Siegfried Brockmann=2C 1958

Arriving at the end of 1963=2C Mrs. Cole bought Kerna Maybeck=E2=80=99s ow=
n house at 2701 Buena Vista and lived there before buying the Studio (also=
 from Kerna) in 1972. Maga had meanwhile put both her architecture degree=
 and her interest in Maybeck=E2=80=99s building philosophy to good use by=
 designing and building her own =E2=80=9CBerkeley=E2=80=9D house on proper=
ty purchased from the Maybecks. The house was completed in 1968 and stands=
 next door to the Studio on the uphill side. Around the same time=2C Maga=
 became actively involved in an architectural history group that later eme=
rged as the Berkeley Architectural Heritage Association.

As the new owner of the Studio=2C Mrs. Cole devoted her time to its needed=
 restoration. Jacomena wrote=2C =E2=80=9Cshe went through it with money an=
d loving care.=E2=80=9D The house had been a rental for many years after t=
he Maybeck family dispersed to other nearby homes. The original=2C fragile=
 Bubblestone roof leaked. Mrs. Cole had a metal roof put on. She made util=
itarian but costly upgrades such as new electrical and plumbing. Whatever=
 she did aesthetically to the house or the garden=2C she asked herself=2C=
 =E2=80=9CWhat would Maybeck do?=E2=80=9D Both the metal tread on the gard=
en steps and the garden lights are Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s touches. (See if yo=
u can find all three of the Maybeck-inspired garden lights when you are at=
 the Sale.)

One of the garden lights designed by Mrs. Cole. Anthony Bruce=2C 2026

Part of the restoration that Mrs. Cole undertook in 1974=E2=80=9376 was to=
 repair and protect the distinctive Bubblestone sack wall covering. In Jun=
e of 1980=2C Richard Rice=2C son of Bubblestone inventor John Rice=2C went=
 on a pilgrimage to determine how Maybeck=E2=80=99s early experiments with=
 the material had held up. At the Studio he met Mrs. Cole and Maga and rec=
orded what had been done: =E2=80=9CIn Berkeley I found the Bubblestone sac=
ks on both the Studio and Cottage=2C as expected=2C much weakened by rotti=
ng of the jute. Mrs. Frances Cole=2C a friend of the Maybecks and now owne=
r of the Studio=2C had worked diligently to strengthen and preserve the sa=
cks. She had found and used a commercial product that had the desired effe=
ct when carefully applied in a thin coating.=E2=80=9D Her contractor had t=
hen recommended painting the sacks for durability. Mrs. Cole chose a color=
 to simulate the original tinted concrete and keyed it to the rosy hue of=
 the fallen eucalyptus leaves that lay all about on the property. The hous=
e will have been
repainted for the first time by the date of the Sale. Creating one paint c=
olor to match the various shades of =E2=80=9CMaybeck Pink=E2=80=9D that co=
-exist on the house is a challenge!

This is how Richard Rice found the Studio after Mrs. Cole's restorative wo=
rk on the sacks. Richard Rice=2C 1980=2C Siegfried Brockmann papers

After the renovations were completed=2C Mrs. Cole sold her house next door=
 and moved into the Studio. She had turned it again into a beautiful and c=
omfortable place too live=2C and=2C throughout=2C the linen-velvet draperi=
es of rich=2C dark red=E2=80=94inspired by Maybeck=E2=80=94hung on the win=
dows and sliding doors.

Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s daughter Maga died at an early age in 1984. Mrs. Cole=
 was left alone=2C without a nearby family member. She did however=2C have=
 another close person who visited her several times a year from St. Louis.=
 His name was Siegfried; he and the Coles had met at a New Year=E2=80=99s=
 Day party in 1950. Siegfried soon became the son that they had never had.=
 Mrs. Cole had always intended the Studio to go to Siegfried=2C and upon h=
er death in the summer of 1989=2C Siegfried found himself the owner of a B=
erkeley treasure.

Mrs. Cole's linen velvet draperies and a few of the sale items.

You will find a number of items for sale in the house that once belonged t=
o Frances Cole=2C such as two Victorian side chairs covered in dark red li=
nen velvet. Mr. Cole will be represented by pristine bottles of a Cole Che=
mical Company product=2C =E2=80=9CBrom.=E2=80=9D You might even discover a=
 relief map of North America made by Maga Cole when she was ten years old.

=E2=80=94Anthony Bruce=2C 2026

Mrs. Cole and Maga in Washington=2C D.C. Walter Brockmann=2C 1950


VOLUNTEERS WANTED

Volunteers are needed to help in different capacities during  the two days=
 of the Sale. Most of Saturday is covered; there are still quite a few shi=
fts open for Sunday

Volunteers receive free admission to the event.

To volunteer=2C
