p;the<br>
Estate Sale House &mdash; in four parts</span></em></strong></span></span>=
<br>
<br>
<span style=3D"color:#ff0066"><span style=3D"font-size:24px"><font face=3D=
"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif"><span style=3D"caret-color:=
rgb(75=2C 0=2C 130)"><strong>Part Two</strong></span></font></span><br>
<br>
<span style=3D"font-size:30px"><font face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new=
 roman=2C serif"><span style=3D"caret-color:rgb(75=2C 0=2C 130)"><strong>&=
quot;Mrs. Cole&quot;</strong></span></font></span></span></p>

<div style=3D"text-align: left;color: #505050;font-family: Arial;font-size=
: 14px;line-height: 150%;">
<p><font color=3D"#000000" face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C=
 serif" size=3D"3">That the Maybeck Studio has survived so&nbsp;<span styl=
e=3D"caret-color:rgb(0=2C 0=2C 0)">beautifully</span>&nbsp;to this day=2C=
 we have the second owner to thank. There have been three owners of the Ma=
ybeck Studio at 2711 Buena Vista Way during its 102 years of existence; al=
l owners have either themselv</font><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span st=
yle=3D"font-size:16px"><span style=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C time=
s new roman=2C serif">es been Maybecks or have been close friends of the M=
aybeck family. Frances van Barneveld Cole (1898&ndash;1989) was the second=
 owner.</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><br>
<img data-file-id=3D"6724991" height=3D"369" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.=
com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/5043f50e-df60-4ca7-b495-b63c8bf1b4b9.=
gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 375px;height: 369px;margin: 0px;line-heig=
ht: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: none;display: inline;max-width: 46=
0px;" width=3D"375"><br>
Frances van Barneveld Cole. Water Brockmann=2C 1950</p>

<p><br>
<span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span style=
=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Her connecti=
on to the Maybecks went back to 1915 when her father was appointed Chief o=
f the Department of Mining and Metallu</span></span></span><span style=3D"=
color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span style=3D"font-family:g=
eorgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">rgy (to be housed in the Pala=
ce of the same name) at the Panama-Pacific International Exposition. Also=
 teaching courses at the University=2C Prof. Van Barneveld moved his famil=
y to Berkeley where they landed in the neighborhood of Annie and Bernard M=
aybeck=2C with the two families becoming friends. The four van Barneveld c=
hildren&mdash;all teenagers&mdash;were close in age to Kerna and Wallen Ma=
ybeck. Frances van Barneveld remembered planting redwood seedlings on the=
 bare hillsides with Kerna and Wallen. The van Barnevelds stayed in Berkel=
ey long enough for the children to attend the University. Daughter Frances=
 then spent most of her adult life in St Louis=2C married to Barzillai Lin=
neas Cole=2C the founder&nbsp;of the Cole Chemical Company=2C a pharmaceut=
ical firm.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Frances Col=
e kept in touch with the Maybecks. In fact=2C the Maybecks stayed with the=
 Coles when Maybeck was asked to design a campus for Principia College acr=
oss the river at Elsah=2C Illinois. Mrs. Cole noticed that Ben came down f=
or breakfast each morning wearing a long velvet bathrobe of a distinctive=
 dark red color. That image stayed in her mind=2C and years later she util=
ized her memory of Maybeck&rsquo;s sartorial preference when she had dark=
 red linen velvet draperies made for the Studio. On one of Maybeck&rsquo;s=
 many visits to St. Louis=2C the Coles commissioned him to design a new bu=
ilding for Cole Chemical Company. The plans and rendering still exist=2C b=
ut a local architect was ultimately chosen for practicality.</span></span>=
</span><br>
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=
=3D"font-size:16px"><span style=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times n=
ew roman=2C serif"><img data-file-id=3D"6724992" height=3D"290" src=3D"htt=
ps://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/58f0be39-82b7-69b5=
-e477-1d61ab05c45e.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;height: 290px;ma=
rgin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: none;display: i=
nline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"></span></span></span><br>
Maga and her parents in Palm Beach=2C c. 1936. Siegfried Brockmann papers<=
/p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">The Coles h=
ad one child: Margaret (1927&ndash;1984)=2C whom her parents were devoted=
 to=2C and who was forever known by her childhood nickname=2C &ldquo;Maga.=
&rdquo; In 1958=2C Maga decided to continue her study of architecture in B=
erkeley. Her mother suggested she call on the Maybecks. Wallen and his wif=
e Jacomena were pleased to meet the next Cole generation (their own twins=
 were the same age as Maga)=2C and they immediately rented their Maybeck T=
win Drive cottage=2C Arillaga=2C to Maga. Mr. Cole died unexpectedly in 19=
63. Mrs. Cole sold the company=2C as well as their St. Louis estate=2C and=
 headed west to be near Maga.</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725042" height=3D"3=
63" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/da41=
0dc1-4e8e-e932-884c-ccd73ea7173d.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 363px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Maga at the time she left for Berkeley. Siegfried Brockmann=2C 1958</p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Arriving at=
 the end of 1963=2C Mrs. Cole bought Kerna Maybeck&rsquo;s own house at 27=
01 Buena Vista and lived there before buying the Studio (also from Kerna)=
 in 1972. Maga had meanwhile put both her architecture degree and her inte=
rest in Maybeck&rsquo;s building philosophy to good use by designing and b=
uilding her own &ldquo;Berkeley&rdquo; house on property purchased from th=
e Maybecks. The house was completed in 1968 and stands next door to the St=
udio on the uphill side. Around the same time=2C Maga became actively invo=
lved in an architectural history group that later emerged as the Berkeley=
 Architectural Heritage Association.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p>

<p><font color=3D"#000000" face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C=
 serif" size=3D"3">As the new owner of the Studio=2C Mrs. Cole devoted her=
 time to its needed restoration. Jacomena wrote=2C &ldquo;she went through=
 it with money and loving care.&rdquo; The house had been a rental for man=
y years after the Maybeck family dispersed to other nearby homes. The orig=
inal=2C fragile Bubblestone roof leaked. Mrs. Cole had a metal roof put on=
=2E She made&nbsp;utilitarian but&nbsp;costly upgrades such as new&nbsp;elec=
trical and plumbing.&nbsp;Whatever she did aesthetically to the house or t=
he garden=2C she asked herself=2C &ldquo;What would Maybeck do?&rdquo; Bot=
h the metal tread on the garden steps and the garden lights are Mrs. Cole&=
rsquo;s touches. (See if you can find all three of the Maybeck-inspired ga=
rden lights when you are at the Sale.)&nbsp;</font></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725043" height=3D"3=
68" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/e76a=
6039-16d6-60b7-9771-bb598c321f90.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 368px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
One of the garden lights designed by Mrs. Cole. Anthony Bruce=2C 2026</p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Part of the=
 restoration that Mrs. Cole undertook in 1974&ndash;76 was to repair and p=
rotect the distinctive Bubblestone sack wall covering. In June of 1980=2C=
 Richard Rice=2C son of Bubblestone inventor John Rice=2C went on a pilgri=
mage to determine how Maybeck&rsquo;s early experiments with the material=
 had held up. At the Studio he met Mrs. Cole and Maga and recorded what ha=
d been done: &ldquo;In Berkeley I found the Bubblestone sacks on both the=
 Studio and Cottage=2C as expected=2C much weakened by rotting of the jute=
=2E Mrs. Frances Cole=2C a friend of the Maybecks and now owner of the Studi=
o=2C had worked diligently to strengthen and preserve the sacks. She had f=
ound and used a commercial product that had the desired effect when carefu=
lly applied in a thin coating.&rdquo; Her contractor had then recommended=
 painting the sacks for durability. Mrs. Cole chose a color to simulate th=
e original tinted concrete and keyed it to the rosy hue of the fallen euca=
lyptus leaves that lay all about on the property. The house will have been=
 repainted for the first time by the date of the Sale. Creating one paint=
 color to match the various shades of &ldquo;Maybeck Pink&rdquo; that co-e=
xist on the house is a challenge!</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725044" height=3D"3=
08" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/0170=
3111-0de5-5210-468b-29360b1458ce.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 308px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
This is how Richard Rice found the Studio after Mrs. Cole&#39;s restorativ=
e work on the sacks. Richard Rice=2C 1980=2C Siegfried Brockmann papers</p=
>

<p><font color=3D"#000000" face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C=
 serif" size=3D"3">After the renovations were completed=2C Mrs. Cole sold=
 her house next door and moved into the Studio. She had turned it again in=
to a beautiful and comfortable place&nbsp;<span style=3D"caret-color:rgb(0=
=2C 0=2C 0)">too</span>&nbsp;live=2C and=2C throughout=2C the linen-velvet=
 draperies of rich=2C dark red&mdash;</font><em style=3D"color:rgb(0=2C 0=
=2C 0); font-family:georgia=2C times=2C &quot;times new roman=2C serif; fo=
nt-size:16px">inspired by Maybeck</em><font color=3D"#000000" face=3D"geor=
gia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif" size=3D"3">&mdash;hung on the wi=
ndows and sliding doors.&nbsp;</font></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Mrs. Cole&r=
squo;s daughter Maga died at an early age in 1984. Mrs. Cole was left alon=
e=2C without a nearby family member. She did however=2C have another close=
 person who visited her several times a year from St. Louis. His name was=
 Siegfried; he and the Coles had met at a New Year&rsquo;s Day party&nbsp;=
in 1950.</span></span> </span><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D=
"font-size:16px"><span style=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new=
 roman=2C serif">Siegfried soon became the son that they had</span></span>=
</span> <span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span=
 style=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">never=
 had. Mrs. Cole had always intended the Studio to go to Siegfried=2C and u=
pon her death in the summer of 1989=2C Siegfried found himself the owner o=
f a Berkeley treasure.</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725045" height=3D"2=
45" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/1b99=
d063-2b28-3864-2698-ff0d689e69bc.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 245px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Mrs. Cole&#39;s linen velvet draperies and a few of the sale items.</p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">You will fi=
nd a number of items for sale in the house that once belonged to Frances C=
ole=2C such as two Victorian side chairs covered in dark red linen velvet.=
 Mr. Cole will be represented by pristine bottles of a Cole Chemical Compa=
ny product=2C &ldquo;Brom.&rdquo; You might even discover a relief map of=
 North America made by Maga Cole when she was ten years old.</span></span>=
</span></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">&mdash;Anth=
ony Bruce=2C 2026</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725046" height=3D"3=
45" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/acd3=
20d0-1be2-59a4-159d-616597c0c9aa.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 345px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Mrs. Cole and Maga in Washington=2C D.C. Walter Brockmann=2C 1950</p>
</div>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;">&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><strong><span style=3D"font-size:18px"><s=
